why: the operator asked for it — Scala 3.9.0 shipped and it opens the new
  Long Term Support line, succeeding 3.3 LTS (three years of maintenance
  guaranteed). This build pins 3.7.4, a non-LTS line that stops getting
  fixes; specs/modules-infra.md's "latest (3.7+), not LTS" was written
  when LTS meant 3.3, i.e. two years behind. 3.9 makes latest and LTS the
  same choice for the first time, so that decision is worth re-taking.

what: move ThisBuild / scalaVersion from 3.7.4 to 3.9.0 across the whole
  build and make the FULL MATRIX green on it — JVM, JS and Native, main,
  test and Jmh, zero warnings. That includes deciding okay-spark's fate:
  build.sbt's own comment says the ceiling is okay-spark and only
  okay-spark, and that it compiles on 3.9.0 once scala-reflect is pinned
  (it now is) but its tests then fail at RUNTIME with "Cannot find a
  SparkSession implementation on the Classpath". Compiling is not the
  bar; the suite is. Rewrite the version comment in build.sbt to whatever
  is then TRUE, and update specs/modules-infra.md's Scala line.

known before starting (dfbd23a7, 2026-08-31, bisected not guessed):
  3.6.4 clean / 3.7.4 clean / 3.8.4 63 errors / 3.9.0 63 errors, and the
  63 were ONE cause — `Free.run`'s named context function type
  `(M: Monad[F]) ?=> F[A]`, whose `M` is out of scope in the body on 3.8+.
  That line was already rewritten to `(using M: Monad[F])` in that commit,
  so the core is expected clean today. Do not re-derive it; verify it.

what 3.7 -> 3.9 brings that this build can trip on:
  - the standard library is now compiled WITH Scala 3 (3.8). Explicit
    arguments to context-bound parameters need `using`
    (`Array.empty(using ClassTag.Int)`). This is also the first suspect
    for okay-spark: a Scala-3-compiled stdlib beside Spark's 2.13
    bytecode under `for3Use2_13`.
  - the REPL moved OUT to a separate `scala3-repl` artifact (3.8). This
    build depends on scala3-compiler (build.sbt:1183) and scala3-staging
    (build.sbt:1482) — check okay-script and okay-staging for a REPL
    reach before assuming they only want the compiler.
  - given search now picks the most GENERAL matching instance, not the
    most specific (3.7). A silent behaviour change, not an error; the
    suite is what catches it. `-source:3.7-migration` names the sites.
  - private implicits in a type's companion warn under -deprecation
    (3.9), an error in 3.10 — and this repo's policy is zero warnings.
  - JDK 17 floor (3.8). .sdkmanrc pins 21, so this is free.
  - Scala.js 1.22 rides along (stable Wasm backend); okay is cross-built
    to JS and Native, so both linkers move under us too.

predict: the core, and most of the 60-odd modules, come up green with the
  version string alone — that is what dfbd23a7 measured and nothing has
  gone backwards since. The two places I expect to spend the session are
  (1) okay-spark's runtime, which may not be fixable inside a version
  bump and may cost the module a `.settings(scalaVersion := "3.7.4")`
  pin or an exclusion, and (2) new warnings under a zero-warning policy,
  most likely from the deprecation in 3.9 and from the stdlib change.
  A silent given-search regression is the outcome I would MISS, so the
  matrix is the gate, not the compile.

CAUTION — MACHINE STATE THIS CLAIM DOES NOT OWN:
  bench-refresh holds the box for JMH measurement and has UNLOADED both
  scalascript launchd guards. A full matrix here perturbs their numbers
  and their forks perturb my build. No heavy compile starts until they
  say the box is free; check `pgrep -f org.openjdk.jmh.runner.ForkedMain`
  and `pgrep -f sbt-launch` before every gate, not once.

