TOOLCHAIN — all three platforms already support 3.9.0 at the plugin
versions plugins.sbt pins. No plugin bump is part of this migration:
  org.scala-native:nscplugin_3.9.0:0.5.12          published (metadata)
  org.scala-native:scala3lib_native0.5_3:3.9.0+0.5.12   published
  org.scala-lang:scala3-library_sjs1_3:3.9.0       published
  sbt-scalajs 1.22.0 is the version 3.9.0 ships with.

okay-script's compiler API SURVIVES. The REPL left the compiler jar in
3.8 (own `scala3-repl` artifact) and this build depends on
scala3-compiler — but ScalaScript.scala uses `dotty.tools.dotc.Driver`,
`StoreReporter` and `Contexts.Context`, none of them REPL. Checked in
the 3.9.0 jar itself: Driver.class and StoreReporter.class are present,
`process(Array[String], Reporter, CompilerCallback)` still overloaded the
same way, and StoreReporter still publishes both constructor defaults
(`$lessinit$greater$default$1/2`), so `new StoreReporter(null)` at
ScalaScript.scala:709 still compiles. No `repl` classes in the jar,
which is the change, and we do not touch them.

okay-spark: THE MECHANISM IS FOUND, and it is not Spark's fault.
build.sbt says only that the tests "fail at RUNTIME"; here is why, and
it explains exactly why 3.7 works and 3.8+ does not:
  - through 3.7 the stdlib on the classpath was `org.scala-lang:
    scala-library:2.13.x` and the Scala-3-only classes lived apart in
    `scala3-library_3`. TWO jars, no conflict with Spark's 2.13 tree.
  - from 3.8 the stdlib is compiled WITH Scala 3 and published as
    `org.scala-lang:scala-library:3.9.0` — same groupId:artifactId as
    Spark's `scala-library:2.13.18`, so one evicts the other, and 3.9.0
    wins on version order. `scala3-library_3:3.9.0` is now an EMPTY
    344-byte shim (manifest only) that just depends on scala-library
    3.9.0 — verified by unzipping it — so the old two-jar arrangement
    cannot be reconstructed.
  - and the two jars are NOT interchangeable for Scala 2 reflection.
    `javap -v scala.collection.immutable.List`:
        scala-library 2.13.18 -> @scala.reflect.ScalaSignature + ScalaSig
        scala-library 3.9.0   -> TASTY + an EMPTY `Scala` attribute
    Scala 3 emits TASTy where Scala 2 emitted pickles, so scala-reflect
    2.13 (which Spark's ScalaReflection/TypeTag encoders use, and which
    this build already pins explicitly at build.sbt) finds no signature
    for any stdlib type at run time. A swallowed failure on that path is
    a very plausible source of the "Cannot find a SparkSession
    implementation on the Classpath" message, which is what Spark 4
    reports when loading its classic SparkSession companion throws.
  Spark 4.2.0 is out (4.0.0 is what we pin) and still 2.13-only —
  `cs resolve` says 4.2.0 wants scala-library/scala-reflect 2.13.18,
  4.0.0 wants 2.13.16. Operator's call: TRY 4.2.0 FIRST on 3.9, since
  a newer Spark may have fixed its own end, and only then decide.
  If it still fails, the one idea worth a try before pinning the module
  back to 3.7.4 is CLASSPATH ORDER rather than eviction: put the
  2.13.18 jar first on okay-spark's test classpath and let the 3.9.0
  one behind it supply only the Scala-3-only classes (CanEqual,
  deriving, quoted, runtime.LazyVals). Delicate, and it must be
  measured, not argued.

WHAT I HAVE NOT CHECKED and cannot without the box: the given-search
change (most general, not most specific — a SILENT behaviour change,
only the matrix catches it), new warnings under the zero-warning
policy, and whether `zio-direct` 1.0.0-RC7 / `kyo-direct` 0.16.2 in
`compare` still expand under a 3.9 macro API. Those two are the
likeliest new breakage and they live in the Jmh configuration, which
`Test/compile` does not reach — gate with `compare/Jmh/compile`.
